\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Quantitative results for the literal cellar branch. (a)--(b) Exposure maps for lengths $79$ and $167$: every admissible prefix coordinate lies inside some left or right boundary window, matching Theorem\nobreakspace  {}\ref {thm:exposure}. (c) On the solved control family, the exact extendable frontier and the matched static frontier coincide for free-tail lengths $10$, $12$, and $14$; the overlaid line at ratio $1$ shows that the static boundary-debt signature is already prefix-identifying. (d) On the eight real-anchor panels, thousands of feasible prefixes are scanned, but the exact-completion, boundary-frontier, and cellar-frontier rows remain identically zero. The top two rows also show that the static signature is injective on the recorded panels, so the no-go cannot be rescued by claiming hidden stack-only classes.\relax }}{21}{figure.caption.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:cellar-results}{{6}{21}{Quantitative results for the literal cellar branch. (a)--(b) Exposure maps for lengths $79$ and $167$: every admissible prefix coordinate lies inside some left or right boundary window, matching Theorem~\ref {thm:exposure}. (c) On the solved control family, the exact extendable frontier and the matched static frontier coincide for free-tail lengths $10$, $12$, and $14$; the overlaid line at ratio $1$ shows that the static boundary-debt signature is already prefix-identifying. (d) On the eight real-anchor panels, thousands of feasible prefixes are scanned, but the exact-completion, boundary-frontier, and cellar-frontier rows remain identically zero. The top two rows also show that the static signature is injective on the recorded panels, so the no-go cannot be rescued by claiming hidden stack-only classes.\relax }{figure.caption.10}{}}
